            <ArticleTitle>Differential diagnoses of conjunctivitis for clinical allergist-immunologists.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>105-14; quiz 114-7, 152</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To provide a clinical overview of the types of conjunctivitis that are encountered by practicing clinical allergist-immunologists.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Published literature in peer reviewed journals found in the National Library of Medicine (PubMed) database using the keywords ocular allergy and/or allergic conjunctivitis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY SELECTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Studies related to ocular allergy and/or allergic conjunctivitis were selected for inclusion in this review.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Four clinical scenarios are presented that mimic frequently encountered inflammatory disorders that present as red eyes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The signs and symptoms associated with the various inflammatory conditions affecting the conjunctiva often overlap and need to be differentiated to maximize care for patients with conjunctivitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
